<cell id="cell-8"><cell_type>markdown</cell_type>## Step 4: Load base model + tokenizer

`state-spaces/mamba-2.8b-hf` ships with the GPT-NeoX tokenizer (vocab 50277, padded to 50280). Do NOT swap to a LLaMA tokenizer — the embedding matrix is tied and re-init would torch all surrogate-warm weights.

`residual_in_fp32=True` is enforced. Disabling it produces NaN losses within ~200 steps on this corpus.</cell id="cell-8">
<cell id="cell-9">from transformers import AutoTokenizer, MambaForCausalLM, MambaConfig

BASE = 'state-spaces/mamba-2.8b-hf'

tokenizer = AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained(BASE, trust_remote_code=True)
# Mamba has no pad_token by default; add one for sample-packing / batching.
if tokenizer.pad_token is None:
    tokenizer.add_special_tokens({'pad_token': '<|pad|>'})

config = MambaConfig.from_pretrained(BASE, trust_remote_code=True)
# Lock the stability-critical flags before model construction.
config.residual_in_fp32 = True
config.fused_add_norm = True
config.use_cache = False  # incompatible with gradient checkpointing

model = MambaForCausalLM.from_pretrained(
    BASE,
    config=config,
    torch_dtype=torch.float16,
    trust_remote_code=True,
)
# Resize for the added pad token (no-op if already at multiple of 8).
model.resize_token_embeddings(len(tokenizer))
model.gradient_checkpointing_enable()
model.to('cuda')

n_params = sum(p.numel() for p in model.parameters())
n_trainable = sum(p.numel() for p in model.parameters() if p.requires_grad)
print(f'\nModel loaded: {BASE}')
print(f'  total params     = {n_params/1e9:.3f} B')
print(f'  trainable params = {n_trainable/1e9:.3f} B (full-FT, not LoRA)')
print(f'  dtype            = {next(model.parameters()).dtype}')
print(f'  residual_in_fp32 = {config.residual_in_fp32}')
print(f'  grad checkpoint  = {model.is_gradient_checkpointing}')

# Quick named_parameters audit — should see in_proj, out_proj, x_proj, dt_proj, A_log, D, conv1d, norm
seen_classes = set()
for name, p in model.named_parameters():
    for cls in ['A_log', 'D', 'conv1d', 'dt_proj', 'x_proj', 'in_proj', 'out_proj', 'embed', 'norm']:
        if cls in name:
            seen_classes.add(cls)
            break
print(f'\nParameter classes seen in named_parameters: {sorted(seen_classes)}')
assert 'A_log' in seen_classes and 'in_proj' in seen_classes, 'Mamba-class params not found — wrong base model?'</cell id="cell-9">
<cell id="cell-10"><cell_type>markdown</cell_type>## Step 5: Build per-layer-class parameter groups

Each Mamba parameter class trains at a different LR per the YAML spec:

| class    | multiplier | effective LR | weight decay |
|----------|------------|--------------|--------------|
| A_log    | 0.1×       | 5e-6         | 0 (no decay) |
| D        | 0.2×       | 1e-5         | 0 (no decay) |
| conv1d   | 0.2×       | 1e-5         | 0.1          |
| dt_proj  | 0.5×       | 2.5e-5       | 0.1 (bias=no)|
| x_proj   | 0.5×       | 2.5e-5       | 0.1          |
| in_proj  | 1.0×       | 5e-5         | 0.1          |
| out_proj | 1.0×       | 5e-5         | 0.1          |
| embed    | 1.0×       | 5e-5         | 0.1          |
| norm     | 1.0×       | 5e-5         | 0 (no decay) |

Rationale: the SSM core (A_log, D, conv1d) was carefully initialized by state-spaces for stable eigenvalue placement on the unit circle. Hammering it at full LR destroys the surrogate's language prior.</cell id="cell-10">
<cell id="cell-11">BASE_LR = 5e-5
WEIGHT_DECAY = 0.1

# Order matters: most specific match first.
LR_MULT_RULES = [
    ('A_log',         0.1),
    ('D',             0.2),
    ('conv1d',        0.2),
    ('dt_proj.bias',  0.5),
    ('dt_proj',       0.5),
    ('x_proj',        0.5),
    ('in_proj',       1.0),
    ('out_proj',      1.0),
    ('norm',          1.0),
]
NO_DECAY = ['norm', 'A_log', 'D', 'dt_proj.bias']

def classify(name):
    for substr, mult in LR_MULT_RULES:
        if substr in name:
            return substr, mult
    # default bucket for embeddings + lm_head + anything else (e.g. backbone.embeddings.weight)
    return 'embed', 1.0

def decays(name):
    for substr in NO_DECAY:
        if substr in name:
            return False
    return True

# Build groups keyed by (mult, decay_bool) so AdamW sees distinct LR groups.
buckets = {}
class_counts = {}
for name, p in model.named_parameters():
    if not p.requires_grad:
        continue
    cls, mult = classify(name)
    dec = decays(name)
    key = (mult, dec)
    buckets.setdefault(key, []).append(p)
    class_counts[cls] = class_counts.get(cls, 0) + p.numel()

param_groups = []
for (mult, dec), params in sorted(buckets.items(), key=lambda kv: -kv[0][0]):
    n = sum(p.numel() for p in params)
    param_groups.append({
        'params': params,
        'lr': BASE_LR * mult,
        'weight_decay': WEIGHT_DECAY if dec else 0.0,
    })
    print(f'  group mult={mult:>4}  decay={"yes" if dec else " no"}  params={n/1e6:>8.2f} M  effective_lr={BASE_LR*mult:.2e}')

print('\nParam class coverage (should sum to ~2.8 B):')
for cls, n in sorted(class_counts.items(), key=lambda kv: -kv[1]):
    print(f'  {cls:<10} {n/1e6:>10.2f} M')
print(f'  TOTAL       {sum(class_counts.values())/1e9:>10.3f} B')</cell id="cell-11">
<cell id="cell-12"><cell_type>markdown</cell_type>## Step 6: Build composite dataset + tokenize + pack

Concatenate the three corpora, shuffle deterministically (seed 42), tokenize to 1024-token blocks. Sample-packing concatenates short examples with EOS separators to hit `sequence_len` exactly — critical for T4 throughput.</cell id="cell-12">
<cell id="cell-13">from datasets import load_dataset, concatenate_datasets

SEQ_LEN = 1024
VAL_FRAC = 0.02

ds_parts = []
for p in REQUIRED:
    d = load_dataset('json', data_files=p, split='train')
    print(f'  loaded {os.path.basename(p):<25} rows={len(d)}')
    ds_parts.append(d)
ds_all = concatenate_datasets(ds_parts).shuffle(seed=42)
print(f'  combined: {len(ds_all)} rows')

# Tokenize. EOS-terminate every row so packing has a clean delimiter.
EOS = tokenizer.eos_token_id
def tokenize_row(batch):
    out_ids = []
    for t in batch['text']:
        ids = tokenizer(t, add_special_tokens=False).input_ids
        ids.append(EOS)
        out_ids.append(ids)
    return {'input_ids': out_ids}

ds_tok = ds_all.map(tokenize_row, batched=True, remove_columns=ds_all.column_names, num_proc=2)

# Pack: flatten all ids then chunk to SEQ_LEN.
def pack(batch):
    flat = []
    for ids in batch['input_ids']:
        flat.extend(ids)
    # chunk
    n_full = len(flat) // SEQ_LEN
    chunks = [flat[i*SEQ_LEN:(i+1)*SEQ_LEN] for i in range(n_full)]
    return {'input_ids': chunks, 'labels': [c.copy() for c in chunks]}

ds_packed = ds_tok.map(pack, batched=True, batch_size=1000, remove_columns=['input_ids'], num_proc=2)
print(f'  packed:   {len(ds_packed)} blocks of {SEQ_LEN} tokens')
total_tokens = len(ds_packed) * SEQ_LEN
print(f'  total training tokens: {total_tokens/1e6:.1f} M')

# Train/val split.
ds_split = ds_packed.train_test_split(test_size=VAL_FRAC, seed=42)
ds_train = ds_split['train']
ds_val = ds_split['test']
print(f'  train={len(ds_train)}  val={len(ds_val)}')</cell id="cell-13">
<cell id="cell-14"><cell_type>markdown</cell_type>## Step 7: Train

3 epochs. micro_batch 1 × grad_accum 32 = effective batch 32 sequences = 32k tokens per optimizer step. paged AdamW 8-bit. cosine LR with 3% warmup, min 10% of base. Eval + save every 500 steps. Keep last 4 checkpoints.

T4 wall-clock estimate: ~10 ms/token forward+backward at seq_len 1024 micro-batch 1 with fused kernels = ~10 sec/step → ~13h for 3 epochs on 10M tokens. Real time varies with corpus size and Triton compile cache.</cell id="cell-14">
<cell id="cell-15">from transformers import Trainer, TrainingArguments
from transformers import DataCollatorForLanguageModeling
import bitsandbytes as bnb

# paged_adamw_8bit, but instantiated against our custom param_groups so per-class LRs stick.
optimizer = bnb.optim.PagedAdamW8bit(
    param_groups,
    lr=BASE_LR,            # per-group LR overrides this, but Trainer reads it for logs
    betas=(0.9, 0.95),
    eps=1e-8,
)

# Cosine scheduler with 3% warmup, 10% min LR floor.
# We let HuggingFace build it via training_args by passing the optimizer; the per-group LRs
# are preserved because the scheduler scales all groups by the same multiplier.
steps_per_epoch = max(1, len(ds_train) // 32)  # effective batch 32
TOTAL_STEPS = steps_per_epoch * 3
WARMUP_STEPS = max(1, int(TOTAL_STEPS * 0.03))
print(f'steps_per_epoch ≈ {steps_per_epoch}  total ≈ {TOTAL_STEPS}  warmup = {WARMUP_STEPS}')

from torch.optim.lr_scheduler import LambdaLR
import math
def lr_lambda(step):
    if step < WARMUP_STEPS:
        return step / max(1, WARMUP_STEPS)
    # cosine to 10% of base
    progress = (step - WARMUP_STEPS) / max(1, TOTAL_STEPS - WARMUP_STEPS)
    cosine = 0.5 * (1 + math.cos(math.pi * min(1.0, progress)))
    return 0.1 + 0.9 * cosine
scheduler = LambdaLR(optimizer, lr_lambda)

training_args = TrainingArguments(
    output_dir=OUTPUT_DIR,
    overwrite_output_dir=False,
    num_train_epochs=3,
    per_device_train_batch_size=1,
    per_device_eval_batch_size=1,
    gradient_accumulation_steps=32,
    gradient_checkpointing=True,
    fp16=True,
    bf16=False,
    tf32=False,
    max_grad_norm=1.0,
    logging_steps=10,
    save_strategy='steps',
    save_steps=500,
    save_total_limit=4,
    eval_strategy='steps',
    eval_steps=500,
    load_best_model_at_end=True,
    metric_for_best_model='eval_loss',
    greater_is_better=False,
    seed=42,
    data_seed=42,
    report_to='none',
    remove_unused_columns=False,
    dataloader_num_workers=2,
    # disable Trainer's own optimizer/scheduler so our paged-AdamW + custom param groups are used
)

# Collator: pad + build labels masking pad tokens.
class PassthroughCollator:
    def __init__(self, tokenizer):
        self.pad_id = tokenizer.pad_token_id
    def __call__(self, batch):
        ids = torch.tensor([b['input_ids'] for b in batch], dtype=torch.long)
        labels = ids.clone()
        labels[labels == self.pad_id] = -100
        return {'input_ids': ids, 'labels': labels}
collator = PassthroughCollator(tokenizer)

trainer = Trainer(
    model=model,
    args=training_args,
    train_dataset=ds_train,
    eval_dataset=ds_val,
    data_collator=collator,
    tokenizer=tokenizer,
    optimizers=(optimizer, scheduler),
)

train_result = trainer.train()
print(f'\nTraining done. metrics: {train_result.metrics}')

# Save final full-weight checkpoint + tokenizer.
final_dir = f'{OUTPUT_DIR}/final'
trainer.save_model(final_dir)
tokenizer.save_pretrained(final_dir)
print(f'Final checkpoint saved → {final_dir}')</cell id="cell-15">
<cell id="cell-16"><cell_type>markdown</cell_type>## Step 8: Hard guards on the saved checkpoint

Full 2.8B Mamba in fp16 = ~5.6 GB of weights. If `model.safetensors` (or the sharded equivalent) is smaller than 5 GB, the save failed (likely OOM on Drive or interrupted). Refuse to proceed to GGUF conversion if guards trip.</cell id="cell-16">
<cell id="cell-17">import hashlib

files = sorted(os.listdir(final_dir))
print(f'Files in {final_dir}:')
for f in files:
    p = os.path.join(final_dir, f)
    sz_mb = os.path.getsize(p) / 1e6
    print(f'  {f:<45} {sz_mb:>10.2f} MB')

# Sum weight shards (handles both single-file and sharded saves).
weight_files = [f for f in files if f.endswith('.safetensors') or f == 'pytorch_model.bin' or f.startswith('model-') and f.endswith('.safetensors')]
total_weight_bytes = sum(os.path.getsize(os.path.join(final_dir, f)) for f in weight_files)
total_weight_gb = total_weight_bytes / 1e9
print(f'\nTotal weight bytes: {total_weight_gb:.2f} GB across {len(weight_files)} files')

if total_weight_gb < 5.0:
    raise RuntimeError(
        f'Final checkpoint weights total {total_weight_gb:.2f} GB — expected ~5.6 GB for 2.8B fp16.\n'
        f'Save likely failed or was interrupted. Check Drive quota and re-run save.'
    )

# Receipt: SHA-256 every weight shard (slow but truthful).
shard_hashes = {}
for f in weight_files:
    h = hashlib.sha256()
    with open(os.path.join(final_dir, f), 'rb') as fp:
        for chunk in iter(lambda: fp.read(1 << 20), b''):
            h.update(chunk)
    shard_hashes[f] = h.hexdigest()
    print(f'  sha256[{f}] = {shard_hashes[f][:32]}...')</cell id="cell-17">
<cell id="cell-18"><cell_type>markdown</cell_type>## Step 9: Convert to GGUF + quantize to Q5_K_M via llama.cpp

Pipeline:
1. `git clone https://github.com/ggerganov/llama.cpp` + build the quantize tool.
2. `python llama.cpp/convert_hf_to_gguf.py <final_dir> --outfile ae-black-mamba-v0-fp16.gguf` — llama.cpp 2024+ supports Mamba HF format natively.
3. `./llama.cpp/build/bin/llama-quantize <fp16.gguf> <Q5_K_M.gguf> Q5_K_M`.

Target Q5_K_M size for 2.8B: ~2.0 GB. Hard fail if output is < 1.5 GB.</cell id="cell-18">
<cell id="cell-19">%%bash
set -e
cd /content
if [ ! -d llama.cpp ]; then
    git clone --depth 1 https://github.com/ggerganov/llama.cpp.git
fi
cd llama.cpp
# Build quantize tool (CPU-only is fine; this isn't a perf-critical path)
pip install -q -r requirements.txt
# llama.cpp moved to CMake in 2024; use that path.
if [ ! -f build/bin/llama-quantize ]; then
    mkdir -p build
    cd build
    cmake .. -DGGML_CUDA=OFF -DLLAMA_BUILD_TESTS=OFF -DLLAMA_BUILD_EXAMPLES=OFF -DLLAMA_BUILD_SERVER=OFF -DLLAMA_CURL=OFF
    cmake --build . --config Release --target llama-quantize -j$(nproc)
fi
ls -lh build/bin/llama-quantize</cell id="cell-19">
<cell id="cell-20">import subprocess

GGUF_FP16 = f'{CORPUS_DIR}/ae-black-mamba-v0-fp16.gguf'
GGUF_Q5KM = f'{CORPUS_DIR}/ae-black-mamba-v0-Q5_K_M.gguf'

# Step 1: HF -> GGUF fp16
print('[convert] HF -> GGUF fp16...')
r = subprocess.run(
    ['python', '/content/llama.cpp/convert_hf_to_gguf.py', final_dir, '--outfile', GGUF_FP16, '--outtype', 'f16'],
    capture_output=True, text=True
)
print('STDOUT:', r.stdout[-2000:] if r.stdout else '(empty)')
if r.returncode != 0:
    print('STDERR:', r.stderr[-3000:])
    raise RuntimeError(f'GGUF conversion failed (rc={r.returncode}). See STDERR above.')
fp16_gb = os.path.getsize(GGUF_FP16) / 1e9
print(f'[convert] fp16 GGUF: {fp16_gb:.2f} GB → {GGUF_FP16}')
if fp16_gb < 4.5:
    raise RuntimeError(f'fp16 GGUF is only {fp16_gb:.2f} GB — expected ~5.6 GB. Conversion lost weights.')

# Step 2: fp16 GGUF -> Q5_K_M
print('\n[quantize] fp16 -> Q5_K_M...')
r = subprocess.run(
    ['/content/llama.cpp/build/bin/llama-quantize', GGUF_FP16, GGUF_Q5KM, 'Q5_K_M'],
    capture_output=True, text=True
)
print('STDOUT:', r.stdout[-2000:] if r.stdout else '(empty)')
if r.returncode != 0:
    print('STDERR:', r.stderr[-3000:])
    raise RuntimeError(f'Quantization failed (rc={r.returncode}).')
q5km_gb = os.path.getsize(GGUF_Q5KM) / 1e9
print(f'[quantize] Q5_K_M GGUF: {q5km_gb:.2f} GB → {GGUF_Q5KM}')
if q5km_gb < 1.5:
    raise RuntimeError(f'Q5_K_M GGUF is only {q5km_gb:.2f} GB — expected ~2.0 GB. Quantization failed.')
if q5km_gb > 3.0:
    print(f'WARN: Q5_K_M is {q5km_gb:.2f} GB — larger than expected ~2.0 GB but proceeding (could be a metadata bloat or vocab issue).')

# Hash the deployable artifact.
h = hashlib.sha256()
with open(GGUF_Q5KM, 'rb') as fp:
    for chunk in iter(lambda: fp.read(1 << 20), b''):
        h.update(chunk)
Q5KM_SHA = h.hexdigest()
print(f'\nQ5_K_M sha256: {Q5KM_SHA}')</cell id="cell-20">
